ncrypt: Keep named keys across runs.

NCryptCreatePersistedKey kept a named key only in memory, and NCryptOpenKey
and NCryptDeleteKey were stubs, so a program never found its key again the
next time it ran. The Epic Games Launcher binds the player's sign-in to such a
key (a DPoP proof is signed with it): it made a new key at every start, and the
server refused the saved sign-in with invalid_dpop_proof.

A named key is now saved when it is finalized, as the private key blob BCrypt
exports, in the registry (HKCU\Software\Wine\NCrypt\Keys, or HKLM with
NCRYPT_MACHINE_KEY_FLAG); NCryptOpenKey loads it and answers NTE_BAD_KEYSET
when there is none, NCryptCreatePersistedKey answers NTE_EXISTS for a name in
use unless NCRYPT_OVERWRITE_KEY_FLAG is given, and NCryptDeleteKey removes it
and frees the handle, as on Windows. RSA and ECDSA P-256 keys, the algorithms
ncrypt creates.

